/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  A FICTITIOUS exothermic liquid reaction:   A + B  ->  P   (in solvent S)

  The process-safety EduTool's teaching reaction (DEV.md 4c C60, second
  review).  It replaced a real esterification that the lesson had been
  running on a known thermodynamic inconsistency (the acetic-acid record's
  two vapour bases, which made its liquid heat of reaction several times too
  large -- that record is its own item in DEV.md C60 and is not touched
  here).  Nothing below is data; every number is an author-set teaching
  assumption, chosen so the lesson shows what it has to show.

  * MASS: 100 + 80 = 180 g/mol (the records' MW), conserved by construction.

  * RATE: first order in B, zero order in A, so the extent rate is k n_B
    and does not depend on the volume (a dosed batchReactor holds its
    declared volume while a charge adds liquid; with this law that changes
    no number).  k = A exp(-Ea/RT) with the values below: about 8e-4 1/s
    at 330 K (a time constant of ~20 min, so a batch is ~95 % converted in
    an hour) and ~0.1 1/s at 400 K -- a runaway that finishes in seconds.

  * HEAT: -60.000 kJ per mol of B, from the four records' formation data
    (the engine prints it as [reaction heat]).  It does NOT depend on
    temperature: Cp_liq(P) = Cp_liq(A) + Cp_liq(B), so dCp = 0 (Kirchhoff),
    and the adiabatic energy balance -- which reads one heat of reaction --
    is the exact first law on these records.  That is why the heat comes
    from formation data and not from a `dH_rxn` override: the override is
    a number the engine cannot cross-check, while the formation data also
    price every transfer (a dose charged into a hot reactor is mixed by
    enthalpy equality) and the props witness's bubble-point run, which
    evaluates the mixture's ideal-gas enthalpy and refuses a species that
    has no formation datum.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

AB_to_P
{
    limitingReactant   reactantB;

    stoichiometry
    (
        { component reactantA;  nu -1;  order 0; }
        { component reactantB;  nu -1;  order 1; }
        { component productP;   nu  1;  order 0; }
    );

    kinetics
    {
        type   Arrhenius;
        A      1.0e8;        // 1/s     author-set
        Ea     7.0e4;        // J/mol   author-set (70 kJ/mol)
    }

    // Heat of reaction: from the records' formation data (no dH_rxn
    // override; see the header).
}
